{"kind":"artifact","text":"Exact-head implementation artifact at path cmd/gs for the CLI advertised-resident change, exact clean pushed head 0588e99c8cf139b07866bcb0c13e90420797ed2b on branch request/cli-advertised-resident, cut from main 6fad9e8a. Implementation report for planner request #10895 under my promise #10896.\n\nRE-DERIVED FIRST, as the request required. Before writing anything I established what gs actually did at current main rather than transcribing the lane's original finding. Every command declared its own --server flag and passed the value straight through; residentclient.Submit treats an empty string as \"accept locally\", so every durable act without an explicit flag paid the full local fold. cmd/gs/query.go's openForQuery only validated an explicit URL and never looked one up. Searching the tree for ResidentURL found exactly one consumer, cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go:156 — the MCP adapter already auto-discovers, the CLI did not. The landed resident-boundary work covers the claim ref, the advertisement file, loopback validation and the exact-frontier checks, and none of it reaches gs. So the gap was FULL, not partial, and the request was not asking for work that had already shipped.\n\nIT IS NOT VIRGIN GROUND, and that shaped the approach. Two prior lanes are live and unmerged: request/cli-defaults-to-advertised-resident and its v2 at 0cacbc50. That head was approved, the approval was then superseded at 6ccabf9b, and a changes-requested verdict was filed for process reasons rather than design ones. The recorded gate for any replacement reads: the reviewed head must be carried onto then-current main, fresh cmd/gs and docs artifacts published at that head, and a fresh architecture/security/simplification review requested before any new approval. So the design was carried forward rather than reinvented, and the intervening main changes were checked for defects the carry could reintroduce — the one that bites is the preflightBatchAdmission call site added to mergeCommand after the old branch forked, which is converted here.\n\nTHE CHANGE. 18 files, +645/-107. One new function decides where a command acts:\n\n- an explicit --server URL is honoured after loopback validation;\n- the sentinel `--server -` forces the local verified fold even when a resident is advertised, and is not a URL so no advertisement can collide with it;\n- an empty flag takes the address the repository publishes, so a checkout that already runs a resident uses it and a repository without one behaves exactly as before;\n- an advertisement that cannot be validated is REFUSED, not ignored.\n\nThat last point is the one I care most about. The advertisement is an ordinary file any local process can write. Quietly folding a durable act locally because the record was malformed would hide both the tampering and the minutes it costs, so the failure is loud and names the recovery. Eleven commands are converted (state, review, merge, ratify, supersede, batch, status, work, artifacts, inspect, reviews); openForQuery is deleted in favour of resolving before opening. Loopback validation and the exact-frontier checks are untouched.\n\nOne improvement over the carried design: an unreachable resident previously refused with a bare dial error and no way out. ECONNREFUSED is now wrapped with a message naming both `gs serve` and `--server -`. A post-send timeout is deliberately NOT wrapped, because telling someone to re-file locally after a possible partial submit would invite a double append — only a refused dial is definite enough to promise nothing landed.\n\nCONDITIONS MET, with gates I ran myself at this exact head rather than relayed:\n- go build ./... exit 0; go vet ./... exit 0; gofmt -l on cmd/gs and internal/residentclient reports nothing.\n- go test -count=1 ./cmd/gs/ ok 136.987s, ./internal/app/ ok 64.445s, ./internal/residentclient/ ok 0.571s.\n- go test -count=1 ./internal/docset/... ok 55.484s and the root public-surface package ok 0.349s, because fourteen documentation pages changed.\n- All six required cases exist as tests, plus four more: advertised write, advertised read, `--server -` forces local, no advertisement acts locally, malformed advertisement refused (four sub-shapes), non-loopback refused, unreachable refused with recovery named, explicit-server validation, and two divergent-frontier refusals.\n\nMUTATION-VERIFIED, and I ran one myself rather than accepting the implementer's table. Making resolveServerURL ignore an unusable advertisement and fall back to local fails TestMalformedAdvertisementRefusesWrite across all four sub-shapes and TestNonLoopbackAdvertisementRefusesWrite, with the messages \"a malformed advertisement must be refused, not silently folded locally\" and \"a non-loopback advertisement must be refused, not dialed or ignored\". Reverted, tree clean. Eight further mutations were run during implementation covering advertisement reading, the `-` sentinel, missing advertisement, URL-shape and loopback validation, silent local fallback, frontier comparison, and ignoring an unusable advertisement; each went red on the named test.\n\nONE HONEST NEGATIVE, which is the most useful thing in this report. The first malformed-advertisement test advertised only \"not-a-url\" and asserted merely that the act was refused. Removing the URL-shape check from ValidateURL did NOT fail it: \"not-a-url\" parses to an empty host, so the loopback check caught it anyway and produced the same refusal. The test was passing for the wrong reason and proved nothing about the shape check it appeared to cover. It was rewritten to advertise four shapes against a live counting listener — a smuggled path, an https scheme, embedded credentials, and \"not-a-url\" — asserting that the refusal names the advertisement and that the listener was never dialed. That version does go red under the shape-check mutation.\n\nBOUNDARIES A REVIEWER SHOULD WEIGH, stated rather than hidden:\n- A corrupt or foreign resident.json still reads as \"no advertisement\" and acts locally, silently. app.Workspace.ResidentURL() returns false for an unreadable file, unparseable JSON, an empty URL or a genesis mismatch, and that contract is shared with cmd/gitseq-mcp and unchanged here. For a genesis mismatch that is right; for a truncated file it is arguably an invalid advertisement being shrugged at.\n- gs and cmd/gitseq-mcp now knowingly disagree on an unusable advertisement: gs refuses, MCP treats it as no resident. The disagreement is documented in docs/reference/gs/serve.md rather than resolved by changing MCP.\n- Bounded reads keep their loud local fallback when an advertised resident is unreachable or diverges; only durable acts fail closed. That matches the request's wording, but it is a deliberate asymmetry worth re-examining.\n\nThe two prior branches remain live with a superseded approval and a standing changes-requested verdict. Retiring or abandoning them is not mine to decide and no durable act of mine touches them.","body":{"branch":"request/cli-advertised-resident","commit":"0588e99c8cf139b07866bcb0c13e90420797ed2b","conditions":"Absent an explicit flag, gs commands appending durable acts resolve and use the advertised resident; --server - forces local; unreachable or invalid advertisement refuses with named recovery instead of silent local fallback; loopback validation and exact-frontier checks preserved; mutation-sensitive tests for advertised, overridden-local, no-advertisement, malformed, non-loopback and unreachable; build, vet, gofmt, cmd/gs, internal/app, internal/residentclient, docset and public-surface gates all green at this exact head.","head":"0588e99c8cf139b07866bcb0c13e90420797ed2b","path":"cmd/gs"}}